1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:02,440
This is Meera.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:05,120
Today was the first day of her chai stall.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,733
She served chai all day, and the galla —

4
00:00:07,787 --> 00:00:10,240
her cash box — is full of notes.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:12,480
But once she pays for the milk, the sugar,

6
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,800
and the stall's rent — did Meera actually

7
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,160
make a profit today?

8
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:17,200
Or a loss?

9
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:19,881
She doesn't know.

10
00:00:20,161 --> 00:00:21,121
And honestly?

11
00:00:21,441 --> 00:00:23,681
Most people who start a business don't either.

12
00:00:24,189 --> 00:00:26,269
By the end of this course, you'll be able

13
00:00:26,349 --> 00:00:28,989
to answer that question — for Meera, and

14
00:00:29,149 --> 00:00:30,429
for your own business.

15
00:00:36,376 --> 00:00:38,376
Accounting sounds scary.

16
00:00:38,616 --> 00:00:40,616
But it started with something you already

17
00:00:40,696 --> 00:00:42,376
know how to do — counting.

18
00:00:43,456 --> 00:00:46,096
Five thousand years ago, in Mesopotamia,

19
00:00:46,176 --> 00:00:48,496
farmers pressed little marks into clay to

20
00:00:48,576 --> 00:00:50,416
count their sheep and grain.

21
00:00:51,176 --> 00:00:53,496
About five hundred years ago, in Venice,

22
00:00:53,576 --> 00:00:56,376
a monk named Luca Pacioli wrote down a clever

23
00:00:56,456 --> 00:00:58,456
system for keeping track of money.

24
00:00:59,136 --> 00:01:01,216
And Indian traders have kept the same kind

25
00:01:01,296 --> 00:01:04,336
of books — the bahi-khata — for centuries.

26
00:01:05,096 --> 00:01:07,416
Today, the books live inside apps.

27
00:01:07,856 --> 00:01:09,736
But the idea hasn't changed.

28
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:12,467
Meet Khata.

29
00:01:12,747 --> 00:01:15,067
Khata is a ledger book, and it'll be your

30
00:01:15,227 --> 00:01:16,547
guide through this course.

31
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:18,287
Notice something?

32
00:01:18,527 --> 00:01:20,687
An open book has two sides.

33
00:01:20,727 --> 00:01:23,327
A left page, and a right page.

34
00:01:24,327 --> 00:01:24,927
Remember that.

35
00:01:25,667 --> 00:01:27,747
It's going to matter more than you think.

36
00:01:28,811 --> 00:01:32,011
Every business owner — a chai stall, a startup,

37
00:01:32,051 --> 00:01:34,651
a giant company — really wants the answers

38
00:01:34,731 --> 00:01:36,411
to just two questions.

39
00:01:37,091 --> 00:01:38,691
One: am I making money?

40
00:01:39,871 --> 00:01:42,591
Two: what do I own, and what do I owe?

41
00:01:44,071 --> 00:01:47,031
Accounting answers both, with two reports.

42
00:01:47,811 --> 00:01:49,571
The first is called the Profit and Loss

43
00:01:49,651 --> 00:01:50,531
statement.

44
00:01:51,011 --> 00:01:52,451
Think of it as a movie.

45
00:01:52,531 --> 00:01:55,091
It plays the story of a period — a day,

46
00:01:55,144 --> 00:01:57,331
a month, a year — and tells you whether

47
00:01:57,451 --> 00:01:59,411
you earned more than you spent.

48
00:02:00,491 --> 00:02:02,411
The second is the Balance Sheet.

49
00:02:02,491 --> 00:02:03,531
That's a photo.

50
00:02:04,111 --> 00:02:05,871
One moment, frozen.

51
00:02:06,711 --> 00:02:09,111
Everything you own, and everything you owe,

52
00:02:09,231 --> 00:02:10,071
right now.

53
00:02:11,151 --> 00:02:12,911
A movie, and a photo.

54
00:02:13,071 --> 00:02:15,151
Profit and Loss, and Balance Sheet.

55
00:02:15,791 --> 00:02:17,831
We'll build both for Meera, by the end of

56
00:02:17,911 --> 00:02:18,551
April.

57
00:02:19,617 --> 00:02:22,577
But first, the most important idea in this

58
00:02:22,737 --> 00:02:24,017
whole lesson.

59
00:02:24,097 --> 00:02:27,217
Meera and Meera's Chai are not the same.

60
00:02:28,167 --> 00:02:30,887
To an accountant, the stall is its own little

61
00:02:30,967 --> 00:02:31,847
person.

62
00:02:32,527 --> 00:02:34,927
It has its own money, its own things, its

63
00:02:35,007 --> 00:02:36,127
own debts.

64
00:02:36,867 --> 00:02:38,827
So when Meera takes fifty thousand rupees

65
00:02:38,907 --> 00:02:41,387
out of her savings and puts it in the galla,

66
00:02:41,467 --> 00:02:43,787
the money now belongs to the stall.

67
00:02:44,427 --> 00:02:46,467
And the stall remembers that it came from

68
00:02:46,547 --> 00:02:47,427
Meera.

69
00:02:48,447 --> 00:02:50,727
Here's where most new founders slip.

70
00:02:51,307 --> 00:02:54,427
It's my stall, so its money is my money.

71
00:02:55,036 --> 00:02:55,556
Nope.

72
00:02:55,756 --> 00:02:57,836
If Meera pays her home electricity bill

73
00:02:57,916 --> 00:03:00,823
from the galla, that's not a stall expense.

74
00:03:01,549 --> 00:03:03,096
And if she buys sugar for the stall with

75
00:03:03,176 --> 00:03:05,496
her own phone, the stall still needs to

76
00:03:05,576 --> 00:03:06,576
know about it.

77
00:03:07,416 --> 00:03:08,269
Two people.

78
00:03:08,623 --> 00:03:09,836
Two sets of money.

79
00:03:10,776 --> 00:03:12,896
This is called the business entity idea,

80
00:03:12,936 --> 00:03:14,936
and every rule you'll learn sits on top

81
00:03:15,016 --> 00:03:15,496
of it.

82
00:03:16,544 --> 00:03:18,784
So what goes into the stall's books?

83
00:03:19,024 --> 00:03:21,664
Only transactions — events that change what

84
00:03:21,704 --> 00:03:24,304
the business has or owes, and that you can

85
00:03:24,384 --> 00:03:25,584
measure in rupees.

86
00:03:26,724 --> 00:03:27,724
Let's play a game.

87
00:03:27,924 --> 00:03:29,204
Is it a transaction?

88
00:03:29,673 --> 00:03:32,553
Meera sells a cup of chai for twenty rupees.

89
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:34,960
Yes!

90
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:37,400
Money came in, chai went out.

91
00:03:38,194 --> 00:03:40,194
Meera smiles at a customer.

92
00:03:41,753 --> 00:03:42,593
Lovely.

93
00:03:42,793 --> 00:03:44,473
But no rupees moved.

94
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:45,993
Not a transaction.

95
00:03:46,559 --> 00:03:47,399
Your turn.

96
00:03:47,679 --> 00:03:49,759
Meera buys a bag of sugar.

97
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:53,600
Yes.

98
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:56,200
Money went out, sugar came in.

99
00:03:56,759 --> 00:03:57,719
And one more.

100
00:03:57,959 --> 00:04:00,279
A milk supplier calls to say hello.

101
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:04,753
Not a transaction.

102
00:04:04,953 --> 00:04:06,433
Nothing changed hands.

103
00:04:06,713 --> 00:04:07,593
Yet.

104
00:04:08,761 --> 00:04:10,881
That's really all accounting is.

105
00:04:11,481 --> 00:04:14,041
You record every transaction, you sort them

106
00:04:14,121 --> 00:04:16,041
into the right buckets, and you sum them

107
00:04:16,121 --> 00:04:17,881
up into the movie and the photo.

108
00:04:19,061 --> 00:04:19,621
Record.

109
00:04:19,941 --> 00:04:20,341
Sort.

110
00:04:20,661 --> 00:04:21,581
Summarise.

111
00:04:22,661 --> 00:04:24,741
Over the next lessons, we'll follow Meera

112
00:04:24,821 --> 00:04:27,701
through her first month — one day at a time.

113
00:04:28,567 --> 00:04:31,127
So — accounting answers two questions.

114
00:04:31,927 --> 00:04:34,167
The movie tells you if you made money.

115
00:04:34,647 --> 00:04:37,367
The photo shows what you own and owe.

116
00:04:37,847 --> 00:04:40,087
And the business is its own person.

117
00:04:41,193 --> 00:04:41,833
Your turn.

118
00:04:41,993 --> 00:04:42,993
Three quick ones.

119
00:04:43,593 --> 00:04:44,113
One.

120
00:04:44,393 --> 00:04:46,313
Meera pays her personal phone bill from

121
00:04:46,433 --> 00:04:47,833
her own bank account.

122
00:04:47,913 --> 00:04:49,833
Does it go in the stall's books?

123
00:04:51,793 --> 00:04:52,353
Two.

124
00:04:52,393 --> 00:04:54,753
A customer asks the price of chai, then

125
00:04:54,833 --> 00:04:55,713
walks away.

126
00:04:56,073 --> 00:04:57,553
Transaction, or not?

127
00:04:59,513 --> 00:05:00,073
Three.

128
00:05:00,153 --> 00:05:02,073
Which report is the photo?

129
00:05:04,113 --> 00:05:06,033
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

130
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:10,200
Next time, Meera opens the galla with fifty

131
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:12,440
thousand rupees of her own savings.

132
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:14,040
So whose money is it now?
